\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Naturality of the fiber interaction}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{b,b'} be \vocabk{selected backgrounds}{fdip-005A} with polynomial
interactions #{\mathcal V,\mathcal V'}. Suppose a
\vocabk{background arrow}{fdip-005C} has vertex counting isometry
#{U:H\to H'} and satisfies #{\mathcal V'\circ U=\mathcal V}.
Then the \vocabk{fiber interactions}{fdip-005Y} obey, to all formal orders,
##{F_{b',\mathcal V'}(\lambda,Ux)
   =F_{b,\mathcal V}(\lambda,x)\qquad(x\in X).}
The assertion also holds for signed endpoint arrows. More generally,
it holds for an orthogonal isomorphism #{O:H\to H'} satisfying
##{Q'=OQO^{-1},\qquad P'=OPO^{-1},\qquad
   \mathcal V'\circ O=\mathcal V,}
with #{Ox} in place of #{Ux}. This latter statement uses the wider
\vocabk{orthogonal comparison}{fdip-005D}, which need not preserve incidence.}

\proof{
\p{It suffices to prove the orthogonal statement. The equation for
#{P'} gives isometries #{O_X:X\to X'} and #{O_Y:Y\to Y'}.
For #{x\in X} and #{y\in Y},
##{\langle O_Xx+O_Yy,Q'(O_Xx+O_Yy)\rangle
   =\langle x+y,Q(x+y)\rangle.}
The change of variables #{y'=O_Yy} has absolute Jacobian one for
the induced Euclidean volumes. Consequently #{Z_{b'}(O_Xx)=Z_b(x)}
and
##{(O_Y)_*\kappa_{b,x}=\kappa_{b',O_Xx}.}
Transport of #{\mathcal V} now gives, for every integer #{n\ge0},
##{\begin{aligned}
&\int_{Y'}\mathcal V'(O_Xx+y')^n\,\kappa_{b',O_Xx}(\extd y')\\
&\qquad=\int_Y\mathcal V(x+y)^n\,\kappa_{b,x}(\extd y).
\end{aligned}}
The integrals are finite. Thus the two moment series agree after
pullback, and so do their formal logarithms. A directed or signed
background arrow supplies the required conjugacy of #{Q,P}, proving
the graph cases as well.}
}

\p{Each coefficient of #{F} is therefore a polynomial transported
by the same retained isometry. Its homogeneous parts correspond by
polarization to symmetric tensors on #{X}. A background automorphism
that also preserves #{\mathcal V} fixes these polynomials and tensors;
choosing coordinates only records their components. Arbitrary
components need not be unchanged by an orthogonal change of basis.
Likewise a graph symmetry need not preserve a supplied interaction.
The invariance assertion uses both the background and interaction
transport equations.}
